# hopf_split: a donut and an hourglass that fit together into a sphere.
#
# The spinors (four numbers, the 3-sphere) split along one torus, where
# |xi| = |eta| (the Clifford torus), into two halves, and each half is a
# solid torus: the inverse of a solid torus, in the 3-sphere, is another
# one, turned inside out through infinity. Carried to ordinary space by
# stereographic projection (hopf_tori), one is the donut, the fibres over
# the northern half of the direction sphere. The other, the fibres over
# the southern half, is a torus too, but enormous: its outer wall is far
# off the screen and closes through infinity, and what is near is its
# inner wall, a narrow waist round the axis through the donut's hole,
# pinched in the middle and flaring above and below: the hourglass. A
# torus's inside is saddle-shaped, and here it is the whole of what you
# see. Glued along the Clifford torus the two are the whole 3-sphere;
# seen from below, the direction sphere's two hemispheres.
#
# Beside them, on the left, the sphere they are drawn from: the direction
# sphere, with its two latitudes. The donut is the fibres over the
# northern one, the hourglass over the southern; as the two latitudes
# move toward the equator their caps grow until they close into the
# whole sphere, and on the right the donut and the hourglass meet as one
# torus, the Clifford torus over the equator. The sphere the tori make
# together is the 3-sphere: the projection spreads it over all of space,
# the donut filling the inside and the hourglass everything outside, out
# through infinity, so it is not a surface to be seen; the sphere on the
# left is the one under it.
#
# Most of the time the two are held apart, the small donut threaded by
# the hourglass's waist; every 40 s they swell to the equator, meet for a
# few seconds, and part again. The circles on each torus are its fibres,
# every one linked with every other, sliding as they go.
#
#   ./aither patches/hopf_split.aither | tools/scope --decay 0.9

let per = 16                                               # fibres round each torus
let passes = 18                                            # the whole picture, a second

# The two latitudes: held near the poles, then once every 40 s swelling
# to the equator (10 s each way, 4 s met) and back; the other the first's
# mirror in the south.
let cyc = t mod 40
let toward = ease((cyc - 14) / 10) * (1 - ease((cyc - 28) / 10))
let sweep = 0.14 + 0.36 * toward                           # a fraction of a half turn: 0.14 .. 0.5

# Each pass: most of the beam on the tori, the rest on the sphere.
let u = (t * passes) mod 1
let on_tori = u < 0.68
let f = u / 0.68 * per * 2                                 # through the fibres
let g = (u - 0.68) / 0.32 * 4                              # through the sphere's four circles
let fibre = floor(f)
let alpha = TAU * (f mod 1)
let south = floor(fibre / per)                             # 0 the donut, 1 the hourglass
let theta = PI * (if south > 0 then 1 - sweep else sweep)
let phi = TAU * (fibre mod per) / per + TAU * t / 37

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the tori

# One direction's spinors, turned together by alpha.
let xi = rotate([cos(theta / 2), 0], alpha)
let eta = rotate([sin(theta / 2), 0], alpha + phi)

# Stereographic projection from where eta is i, which lies on the south
# pole's fibre: that fibre is the axis itself, through infinity.
let away = 1 - eta[1]
let turned = rotate([xi[0] / away, xi[1] / away], TAU * t / 31)
let tipped = rotate([turned[1], eta[0] / away], 1.35)
let near = amplitude([turned[0], tipped[0]]) * 0.13 < 0.55  # the hourglass's far side, through infinity,
let tori = if near then [turned[0] * 0.13 + 0.38, tipped[0] * 0.13] else [3, 3]   # off the screen, where the beam blanks

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the sphere

# The direction sphere, seen at the same tip: its outline, the equator
# (where the Clifford torus sits), and the two latitudes now drawn.
let ring = floor(g)
let a = TAU * (g mod 1)
let lat = if ring == 1 then PI / 2 else if ring == 2 then PI * sweep else PI * (1 - sweep)
let on_rim = [cos(a), sin(a)]                              # ring 0: the outline, flat to the screen
let round = rotate([sin(lat) * cos(a), sin(lat) * sin(a)], TAU * t / 31)
let seen = rotate([round[1], cos(lat)], 1.35)
let dot = if ring == 0 then on_rim else [round[0], seen[0]]
let sphere = [dot[0] * 0.3 - 0.6, dot[1] * 0.3]

if on_tori then tori else sphere
